1460=3D
1461=Enable 3D
1462=Render resolution per eye
1463=The picture's own resolution
1464=Display Configuration...
1465=Swap left and right eyes
1466=Display Configuration could not be opened: kcmshell6 could not be started ({0}).\nOpen System Settings and choose Display Configuration, where each screen's 3D mode and eye swap are.
1467=Display Configuration closed with an error (kcmshell6 exit code {0}).\nOpen System Settings and choose Display Configuration, where each screen's 3D mode and eye swap are.
2100=This file type has no place for a 3D layout. sView writes the layout into JPEG files, as a JPS descriptor, and into PNG files, as an sTER chunk.\nYour choice stays with this file in sView.
2101=Each eye is its own picture in this file, and the file's own tags give their order, which sView does not write.\nYour choice stays with this file in sView.
2102=This pair comes from two files, so there is no single file to write the 3D layout into.\nYour choice stays with these files in sView.
2103=The JPS descriptor cannot describe the layout {0}: it describes side by side, over/under and interlaced rows, each in either eye order, and the red-cyan anaglyph.\nYour choice stays with this file in sView. Media > Save Image As... saves the pair as a new side by side JPS file.
2104=This JPEG is lossless, so it has no place for the 3D descriptor: the JPS section follows the quantization table, and a lossless JPEG has none.\nYour choice stays with this file in sView. Media > Save Image As... saves the pair as a new side by side JPS file.
2105=sView could not read {0} again to write the 3D layout into it.\nYour choice stays with this file in sView.
2106=sView could not write {0}: {1}.\nCheck that the file and its folder can be written, then save again. Your choice stays with this file in sView.
2107=A PNG file describes only side by side pictures, in either eye order, with its sTER chunk, so the layout {0} cannot be written into it.\nYour choice stays with this file in sView. Media > Save Image As... saves the pair as a new side by side JPS file.
2108=The sTER chunk cannot describe this PNG as it is: the picture is {0} pixels wide, so its right view starts at column {1}, and the chunk needs a column divisible by 8. Other programs would split the picture in the wrong place.\nYour choice stays with this file in sView. Media > Save Image As... saves the pair as a new side by side JPS file.
2109=PNG marks only stereo pictures, with the sTER chunk, and this file has none to remove, so a 2D layout cannot be written into it.\nYour choice stays with this file in sView.
2110=sView could not read {0} as a PNG file it can rewrite safely, so the file was left as it was.\nYour choice stays with this file in sView.
2111=The file now carries the 3D layout {0}.

2112=This MPO is not a complete indexed two-view disparity pair with valid viewpoint and base-viewpoint fields. The original file was kept.\nYour choice stays with this file in sView. Media > Save Image As... saves the pair as a new side by side JPS file.
2113=The JPS descriptor is invalid or ambiguous, or contains fields that cannot be preserved safely. The original file was kept.\nYour choice stays with this file in sView. Media > Save Image As... saves the pair as a new side by side JPS file.
2114=The temporary picture did not read back with the chosen layout and eyes. The original file was kept.\nYour choice stays with this file in sView. Media > Save Image As... saves the pair as a new side by side JPS file.
2115=MPO stores separate disparity views and cannot describe the chosen packing. The original file was kept.\nYour choice stays with this file in sView. Media > Save Image As... saves the pair as a new side by side JPS file.
2116=The chosen JPS packing and separation do not fit the stored pixel dimensions. The original file was kept.\nYour choice stays with this file in sView. Media > Save Image As... saves the pair as a new side by side JPS file.
2117=The JPEG segments or scan data are truncated or invalid. The original file was kept.\nYour choice stays with this file in sView. Media > Save Image As... saves the pair as a new side by side JPS file.
2118=The JPEG has unindexed trailing data whose offsets cannot be preserved by a metadata-only save. The original file was kept.\nYour choice stays with this file in sView. Media > Save Image As... saves the pair as a new side by side JPS file.

2119=Lossless JPEG cannot yet be verified by the picture reader; the file was kept.\nYour choice stays with this file in sView. Media > Save Image As... saves the pair as a new side by side JPS file.
